  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyToast` component fails to communicate its dynamic content to assistive technology and lacks programmatic state for toast type. While it implements a basic visual auto-dismiss timer, it omits the core ARIA live region pattern required for screen reader announcement, provides no manual dismissal for keyboard users, and relies on visual-only cues for message type. The design requires structural and behavioral fixes before shipping.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reviewing the code, I predicted: (1) Missing `aria-live` or `role=\"alert\"` on the toast container, causing silent rendering to screen readers; (2) No programmatic association of toast type (success/error) to the message; (3) Lack of manual dismiss mechanism leaving keyboard users with no control over auto-dismissing content; (4) Potential color-only type indicator violating non-text contrast rules. All predictions materialized during investigation.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Toast message not announced to assistive technology**\n   - Evidence: `return (\\n    <div className=\"toast-notification\">\\n      {message}\\n    </div>\\n  );` (Lines 18-22)\n   - User Group Impacted: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages; WAI-ARIA APG Live Regions pattern\n   - Expected Behavior: Dynamic content must be announced immediately upon insertion into the DOM using `aria-live=\"assertive\"` or `role=\"alert\"`.\n   - Fix: Add `role=\"alert\"` and `aria-live=\"assertive\"` to the `<div>`. If multiple toasts can stack, use a single global live region that updates, or ensure each toast mounts in a way that triggers announcement without duplication.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **No manual dismissal mechanism for keyboard users**\n   - Evidence: `useEffect` timer auto-dismisses via `setIsVisible(false)` with no close button or interactive element (Lines 6-14)\n   - User Group Impacted: Keyboard-only users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide; WAI-ARIA APG Alert Dialog pattern\n   - Expected Behavior: Users must be able to pause, stop, or hide auto-updating content. A dismiss button (e.g., `<button aria-label=\"Close notification\">`) should be included and focusable.\n   - Fix: Add a close button inside the toast. Ensure it has `aria-label=\"Close\"` or equivalent visible text, and that clicking it clears the timer (`clearTimeout(timerRef.current)`) and sets `isVisible(false)`.\n\n2. **Toast type (success/error) not communicated programmatically**\n   - Evidence: Component signature `({ message, duration = 3000 })` lacks a `type` prop; expected behavior mentions \"Visual indicator shows toast type\" but code has none (Line 2)\n   - User Group Impacted: Screen reader users, low vision users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 1.4.1 Non-text Contrast; WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n   - Expected Behavior: Toast type must be conveyed via programmatic attributes (e.g., `aria-label=\"Success notification\"` or `role=\"status\"`) alongside visual cues, not color alone.\n   - Fix: Add a `type` prop to the component signature. Pass it to the container: `<div role=\"alert\" aria-live=\"assertive\" aria-label={`${type} notification`}>`. Ensure visual type indicators use shape/icon + text, not color only.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Timer reference lost on re-render**: `const timer = setTimeout(...)` inside `useEffect` creates a new variable each render. If the component updates before unmounting, the cleanup `clearTimeout(timer)` may clear the wrong reference or miss the active one. Use a `useRef` for the timer ID to ensure reliable cleanup and manual dismissal control.\n- **Focus management on mount**: While toasts typically don't steal focus, if this toast is rendered as an overlay/dialog, screen readers need to know whether focus should move to it (`aria-modal=\"true\"` + programmatic focus) or stay in place (standard live region behavior). Clarify the mounting strategy.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Add `aria-atomic=\"true\"` to ensure the full message is announced even if only part updates.\n- Consider using a dedicated toast manager/context instead of inline state to prevent race conditions when multiple toasts mount simultaneously.\n- Ensure the toast container does not interfere with reading order; live regions should be placed near the trigger or at the top/bottom of the viewport without breaking DOM flow expectations.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Missing `aria-live` region**: The component renders dynamic content but relies on static DOM insertion. Screen readers will not announce it unless the user is actively navigating to it or the browser fires a synthetic event (unreliable).\n- **Missing programmatic type association**: Expected behavior specifies visual type indicators, but no `type` prop or ARIA label exists to communicate success/error/warning states to assistive technology.\n- **Missing manual control / timer ref**: No way for users to dismiss early; timer reference is not persisted across renders, risking memory leaks or failed cleanup.\n- **Missing mounting strategy clarification**: It's unclear if this toast mounts synchronously (announced on page load) or asynchronously (announced after interaction). Synchronous live regions can be ignored by AT; asynchronous requires `aria-live` to trigger properly.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No `aria-live`/`role=\"alert\"`; type not announced; silent mount |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | No dismiss button; auto-dismiss only leaves no control |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Expected visual type indicator likely relies on color (non-text contrast risk) |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | No manual close; unpredictable dismissal timing without user control |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails the core accessibility job of a toast: announcing dynamic status messages to assistive technology. Without `aria-live=\"assertive\"` or `role=\"alert\"`, screen reader users will never hear the notification. Combined with missing programmatic type communication and no manual dismissal, the design creates a silent, uncontrollable experience for AT users. These are not compliance edge cases; they are fundamental pattern violations per WAI-ARIA APG Live Regions and WCAG 4.1.3/2.2.2. The component must be revised to implement proper live region semantics, add manual dismissal with timer control, and programmatic type association before it can ship.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- How is this toast mounted? Is it rendered synchronously on page load or asynchronously after a user action? Synchronous mounting of `role=\"alert\"` often fails to announce; asynchronous requires explicit live region wiring.\n- Does the parent application handle multiple concurrent toasts? If so, does each toast get its own live region, or is there a global toast queue that updates a single announcement region?\n- What visual type indicators are planned? If they rely on background/border color changes, do they meet 3:1 non-text contrast against adjacent colors, and are they paired with icons/text for WCAG 1.4.1 compliance?",
